The classical Hopf-Poincaré theorem affirms that the sum of indices of zeros of $V$ is equal to the Euler characteristic of $M$:
\begin{equation}
\sum_{z \in Z(V)} ind_{z}(V) = \chi(M),
\label{eq:1}
\end{equation}
where $Z(V)$ is the set of zeros of $V$ and $ind_{z}(V)$ is the index of $V$ at $z$ (see for example~\cite{bott_tu1982}).

\begin{equation}
\sum_{z \in Z(V)} ind_{z}(V) = \chi(M),
\label{eq:1}
\end{equation}

If, in addition, the manifold $M$ is endowed by a Riemannian metric $g$, then the Gauss-Bonnet theorem says that the Euler characteristic is expressed in terms of the curvature $K$ of this metric:
\begin{equation}
\chi(M) = \frac{1}{2\pi} \int_M K d\sigma,
\label{eq:2}
\end{equation}
where $d\sigma$ is the area form of $g$.

\begin{equation}
\chi(M) = \frac{1}{2\pi} \int_M K d\sigma,
\label{eq:2}
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
\sum_{z \in Z(V)} ind_{z}(V) = \frac{1}{2\pi} \int_M K d\sigma,
\label{eq:3}
\end{equation}

