where $p_F : U \times F \to F$ is the canonical projection onto $F$.
For each $x \in U$ the map $\eta$ restricted to $F_x = \pi^{-1}(x)$ induces a diffeomorphism $\eta_x : F_x \to F$.
Note that
if we take another chart $(U', \psi': \pi^{-1}(U') \to U' \times F)$, and $\eta' : \pi^{-1}(U') \to F$ is the corresponding map, then on $\pi^{-1}(U \bigcap U')$ we have that
\begin{equation}
\psi' \circ \psi^{-1} : (U \cap U') \times F \to (U \cap U') \times F, \quad (x, y) \mapsto (x, g(x) y),
\label{eq:7}
\end{equation}
where $g : U \cap U' \to G$ is the gluing map of the charts.
Now, for any $x \in U \cap U'$, we have $\eta'_x \circ \eta_x^{-1} (y) = g(x) y$, and, as $G$ is connected, $\eta'_x \circ \eta_x^{-1} : F \to F$ is homotopic to the identity map.
